Skip the widget property chain for opaque backgrounds

The event notify callback of the plugin runs for every event of every
object of the process. For a widget without a native window it calls
prePolish, which builds the widget property holder and reads it. The
read runs QObject::property on a 31 character name. It then adds an
index lookup of the meta object and two copies. It creates the property
on the first touch.

The body of prePolish does work only when one of bgndOpacity,
dlgOpacity and menuBgndOpacity differs from 100. prePolish now tests
the three option values first, and it returns before it touches the
property holder. The update request branch of the callback takes the
same guard. It reads the option values of the style of the widget on
each call, so a style swap cannot leave a stale test. A widget with
another style keeps the reset.

The property stays lazily created by the other holder sites. Every
writer of the opacity field writes 100 while the three options equal
100, and the field starts at 100. A configuration with an active
opacity option runs the chain as before.

The measurement takes perf stat -e instructions:u over twenty
subtraction pairs per arm. Each pair sends a scripted trace of 1000
mouse moves to the offscreen widget gallery. The result is 14 981
instructions per move before and 8 466 after, a gain of 43.5 percent.
The first frame of a 500 widget window drops from 1032 to 780 million
instructions. The two builds give a byte equal gallery render over
seven option configurations.

The change sits on the branch work/perf of the local tree, as commit 1 of 1.
A new merge request on system/qtcurve takes that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

diff --git a/qt6/style/prepolish.cpp b/qt6/style/prepolish.cpp
index 02c1a89f..3266b7c5 100644
--- a/qt6/style/prepolish.cpp
+++ b/qt6/style/prepolish.cpp
@@ -31,7 +31,7 @@ namespace QtCurve {
 __attribute__((hot)) void
 Style::prePolish(QWidget *widget) const
 {
-    if (!widget)
+    if (!widget || !qtcOpacityActive(opts))
         return;
     QtcQWidgetProps props(widget);
     // HACK:
diff --git a/qt6/style/qtcurve.h b/qt6/style/qtcurve.h
index 92ce90ce..61e3277e 100644
--- a/qt6/style/qtcurve.h
+++ b/qt6/style/qtcurve.h
@@ -66,6 +66,19 @@ class ShortcutHandler;
 class ShadowHelper;
 class StylePlugin;
 
+//! check whether one of the three background opacity options is active
+/*! Each of the three options holds a percent value, and 100 percent is
+    opaque.
+    \param opts the option set of a style instance.
+    \return true when bgndOpacity, dlgOpacity or menuBgndOpacity differs from
+    100. */
+QTC_ALWAYS_INLINE static inline bool
+qtcOpacityActive(const Options &opts)
+{
+    return (opts.bgndOpacity != 100 || opts.dlgOpacity != 100 ||
+            opts.menuBgndOpacity != 100);
+}
+
 class Style: public ParentStyleClass {
     Q_OBJECT
     Q_CLASSINFO("X-KDE-CustomElements", "true")
diff --git a/qt6/style/qtcurve_plugin.cpp b/qt6/style/qtcurve_plugin.cpp
index 116d98b0..f94fcc23 100644
--- a/qt6/style/qtcurve_plugin.cpp
+++ b/qt6/style/qtcurve_plugin.cpp
@@ -107,8 +107,13 @@ qtcEventCallback(void **cbdata)
             style->prePolish(widget);
         }
     } else if (widget && event->type() == QEvent::UpdateRequest) {
-        QtcQWidgetProps props(widget);
-        props->opacity = 100;
+        // A widget with another style keeps the reset, because QtCurve cannot
+        // read the option set of that style.
+        Style *style = getStyle(widget);
+        if (!style || qtcOpacityActive(style->options())) {
+            QtcQWidgetProps props(widget);
+            props->opacity = 100;
+        }
     } else {
         polishQuickControl(receiver);
     }
